%I A399890 #38 Nov 19 2026 17:00:52
%S A399890 1,1,1,3,5,13,25,59,133,297,685,1599,3645,8537,19949,46403,108801,
%T A399890 255445,598085,1407035,3307489,7775777,18309177,43118687,101518521,
%U A399890 239269665,563888953,1329123715,3133765117,7389708813,17426166929,41102831419,96949491517,228696862409
%N A399890 Number of Dyck paths of semilength n with distinct peak heights and distinct valley heights.
%C A399890 A Dyck path is counted if all its peak heights are distinct and all its valley heights are distinct. A peak and a valley are allowed to have the same height.
%C A399890 A peak occurs at an occurrence of UD, and a valley occurs at an occurrence of DU. The height is the height of the vertex between the two steps.
%C A399890 A valid path has at most two prime (irreducible) components. Three or more components would produce at least two valleys at height 0, which is not allowed.
%C A399890 Let p(n) denote the number of valid prime Dyck paths of semilength n. Removing the first U and the last D gives a valid Dyck path of semilength n-1. Hence p(n) = a(n-1) for n >= 1.
%C A399890 All terms are odd. Reversing the step sequence and interchanging U and D preserves the conditions and pairs the paths. The only fixed path satisfying the conditions is U^n D^n. Therefore a(n) is odd for every n >= 0.
%C A399890 The sequence is strictly increasing for n >= 3.
%C A399890 Let D(i) be the set of valid Dyck paths of semilength i. Let H_p(P) and H_v(P) denote the sets of peak heights and valley heights of P, respectively.
%C A399890 For a nonempty path P, K(P) is the set of peak heights of P. For the empty path, K(P) = {0}. I(P,Q) is 1 if K(P) and K(Q) have no common element and the valley-height sets of P and Q have no common element. Otherwise, I(P,Q) is 0.
%H A399890 Alois P. Heinz, <a href="/A399890/b399890.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..2684</a>
%H A399890 Sean A. Irvine, <a href="https://github.com/archmageirvine/joeis/blob/master/src/irvine/oeis/a399/A399890.java">Java program</a>
%F A399890 a(0) = a(1) = 1.
%F A399890 For n >= 2, a(n) = a(n-1) + q(n), where q(n) = Sum_{i=0..n-2} Sum_{P in D(i)} Sum_{Q in D(n-2-i)} I(P,Q), where I(P,Q) = [K(P) intersect K(Q) = empty set] * [H_v(P) intersect H_v(Q) = empty set] and where K(P) = {0} if P is empty; otherwise K(P) = H_p(P).
%e A399890 For n = 3, there are 5 Dyck paths in total. Exactly 3 of them have pairwise distinct peak heights and pairwise distinct valley heights.
%e A399890 These paths are:
%e A399890   UUUDDD
%e A399890   UUDDUD
%e A399890   UDUUDD
%e A399890 Therefore a(3) = 3.
%o A399890 (Python)
%o A399890 from functools import lru_cache
%o A399890 @lru_cache(None)
%o A399890 def count(u, d, h, last, peaks, valleys):
%o A399890     if u == 0 and d == 0:
%o A399890         return int(h == 0)
%o A399890     total = 0
%o A399890     if u > 0:
%o A399890         if last != 2 or not (valleys & (1 << h)):
%o A399890             v = valleys | ((1 << h) if last == 2 else 0)
%o A399890             total += count(u-1, d, h+1, 1, peaks, v)
%o A399890     if d > 0 and h > 0:
%o A399890         if last != 1 or not (peaks & (1 << h)):
%o A399890             p = peaks | ((1 << h) if last == 1 else 0)
%o A399890             total += count(u, d-1, h-1, 2, p, valleys)
%o A399890     return total
%o A399890 for n in range(22):
%o A399890     print(n, count(n, n, 0, 0, 0, 0))
